WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.040
OK, I've gotten a ton of requests to talk
about console culture, so I want to dig into

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.639
it today and spend some time talking about
this.

00:00:08.639 --> 00:00:14.980
If you haven't heard of cancel culture, cancel
culture is a term that's really all it is

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:22.050
to refer to what is essentially calling someone
out for doing something objectionable.

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:23.050
Right.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:28.980
Canceling your subscription, tweeting about
it, telling an advertiser not to buy a product.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:33.950
Telling Hollywood not to hire a certain actor
or whatever the case may be.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:36.010
It's a type of activism, essentially.

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:40.360
And I really want to repeat this because I
think it's important for framing the entire

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:41.870
conversation.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:47.350
We should not talk about cancel culture as
the thing that is new.

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:52.600
What is new is that it's been named cancel
culture and it's been dubbed by some a major

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:54.300
problem in society.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:58.830
And then we can discuss whether it really
is a major problem in society or not.

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:04.350
So my starting point for talking about cancel
culture is first to ask, does this exist?

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:07.280
Is this really a thing that exists?

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:10.570
Then we ask questions like, is it widespread?

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:13.000
Is it growing for some particular reason?

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.960
At this point in time, once we figure that
out, then we can say, well, is it a problem?

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.880
Is this a bad thing?

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:23.659
And then lastly, we discuss what, if anything,
should be done about it.

00:01:23.659 --> 00:01:24.659
Right.

00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:28.000
We actually want to go through this in the
right way as opposed to doing what some on

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.439
the right and maybe a few people on the left,
but mostly on the right are saying cancel

00:01:32.439 --> 00:01:37.850
culture has exploded among the WOAK left suppression
of speech a little a.

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:38.850
OK.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:39.850
We're not going to do it that way.

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:40.850
We're actually going to go through it.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:43.119
So is cancel culture a real thing?

00:01:43.119 --> 00:01:44.119
Yes, it is.

00:01:44.119 --> 00:01:45.119
Sure it is.

00:01:45.119 --> 00:01:50.639
It is true that sometimes people will boycott
or try to get other people to boycott or try

00:01:50.639 --> 00:01:56.889
to get advertisers to pull ads or get people
to unfollow someone on social media or whatever

00:01:56.889 --> 00:02:05.579
the case may be, because there is the belief
or claim that a person has done or said something

00:02:05.579 --> 00:02:06.740
objectionable.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:10.700
People do things that are perceived as objectionable
all the time.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:13.930
Different people interpret it as objectionable
or not.

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:16.540
And then some people decide to just go on
with their day.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:17.910
Others decide to tweet about it.

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:20.830
Others decide to call advertisers whatever
it exists.

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:22.810
That is not new.

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:25.650
1933, maybe an extreme example.

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:32.210
The American Jewish Congress said Germany
as an entity is now anti-Semitic.

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:33.790
Boycott Germany.

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:40.080
That is something which could be called cancel
culture, supposedly starting in 1973.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:48.220
Various LGBT groups said Coors Brewing Company
has anti-gay hiring practices.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:51.650
Do not buy Coors beer up.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:53.170
Scary cancel culture, right?

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:56.150
I mean that we're talking about very similar
things.

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:58.020
Now, later we'll get into.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:00.520
We don't want this person speaking at our
college.

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:01.920
We're gonna get to that.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:02.920
OK.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:04.670
But the point is, this is not new.

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:07.600
It's activism that's been around for a really
long time.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:08.600
Some people like it.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:09.600
Some people don't.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.430
Their second question.

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:15.990
Is it widespread and is it more widespread
today, for example?

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:18.710
Well, that really depends on what you mean.

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:23.510
You'll hear people on the right who claim
to be against council culture say, you know,

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:29.610
the hashtag, cancel whatever was used eleven
D billion times or something.

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:30.610
Right.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:32.570
OK, well, we have social media now.

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:34.230
It's bigger than it was three years ago.

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:36.010
It's bigger than it was six years ago.

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:42.570
The fact that a particular hashtag was used
a bunch of times three months ago doesn't

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:50.710
really tell us in any empirical way whether
cancel culture has reached epidemic proportions.

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:57.550
What I do see is people realizing that money
is at the root of much of our economy and

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:03.100
using economic threats to try to do activism
may be useful.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:05.000
And that's where ad boycotts.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:10.740
Come from and we can have a debate about whether
that's a useful or effective type of activism.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:17.129
What we have to point out is that just because
something exists and you give it a name doesn't

00:04:17.129 --> 00:04:20.699
mean that it's any more pervasive than it
was before.

00:04:20.699 --> 00:04:27.419
Social media may provide a more convenient
means for people to say, don't buy this or

00:04:27.419 --> 00:04:30.770
don't watch that show or whatever the case
may be.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:36.150
But just because we now have dubbed it cancel
culture doesn't mean it's actually more of

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:38.479
a of an epidemic, so to speak.

00:04:38.479 --> 00:04:43.819
I'll give you an example that many of you
will remember the regressive left.

00:04:43.819 --> 00:04:49.520
Remember when that term was floating around,
you had some people on the left and the right

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:54.300
using this term regressive left than we did
a very we did a deep dove video about it.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:57.129
The left and right used the term differently.

00:04:57.129 --> 00:05:03.560
The left, when they said regressive left,
usually meant when when they when they want

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:09.189
to point to someone who claims to be on the
left but is doing something that's not really

00:05:09.189 --> 00:05:10.189
left.

00:05:10.189 --> 00:05:17.099
So, for example, you might have leftism that
is really authoritarianism, but it's being

00:05:17.099 --> 00:05:19.210
hidden as leftism.

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:24.800
That would be an example of the left being
regressive rather than progressive.

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:30.509
Now the right chord onto the term and started
using it to refer to just anything they don't

00:05:30.509 --> 00:05:31.509
like about the left.

00:05:31.509 --> 00:05:33.120
They're like, oh, that's the regressive left.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:34.370
Single payer health care.

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:35.669
That's the regressive left.

00:05:35.669 --> 00:05:37.759
You know, reforming policing.

00:05:37.759 --> 00:05:38.919
That's the regressive left.

00:05:38.919 --> 00:05:41.370
So the reason regressive left is a good analogy.

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:47.340
Here is a liberalism on the left existed for
a very long time.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:50.819
At some point, the term progressive left was
created.

00:05:50.819 --> 00:05:57.870
And the fact that it was given a nice sounding
name, made it in some people's minds seem

00:05:57.870 --> 00:05:59.789
like it was more prevalent than it actually
was.

00:05:59.789 --> 00:06:02.370
A regressive leftism has been around for a
long time.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:08.449
Go to the early 20th century and you see plenty
of regressive leftism in a number of authoritarian

00:06:08.449 --> 00:06:09.949
countries around the world.

00:06:09.949 --> 00:06:14.349
So you put a catchy name on it and all of
a sudden you've got articles and interviews

00:06:14.349 --> 00:06:17.710
and entire shows devoted to exposing the regressive
left.

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:19.460
The reality was it always existed.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:21.059
I've always called it out on the show.

00:06:21.059 --> 00:06:23.469
You put a name on it doesn't really change
it.

00:06:23.469 --> 00:06:27.659
That gets us back to cancel culture.

00:06:27.659 --> 00:06:32.610
Is cancel culture a problem or has it just
been given a name?

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:33.710
Now, that is catchy.

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:38.750
And so a lot of people are writing about and
talking about cancel culture.

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:41.819
Let me skip the name they're using for a moment.

00:06:41.819 --> 00:06:42.819
Right.

00:06:42.819 --> 00:06:50.910
Cancel culture implies that there is some
widespread culture of canceling stuff, canceling

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.599
people, boycotting, you know, whatever the
case may be.

00:06:53.599 --> 00:06:57.000
The reality is this is called boycott activism.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:59.499
That's what if we unload the term.

00:06:59.499 --> 00:07:01.770
What we're talking about is boycott activism.

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:05.580
This is how capitalism and the marketplace
of ideas works.

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:08.589
Advertisers can advertise on any show they
want to for any reason.

00:07:08.589 --> 00:07:11.219
They can tell us what the reasons are or not.

00:07:11.219 --> 00:07:16.729
Viewers can choose to buy a product for any
reason or choose not to buy a product for

00:07:16.729 --> 00:07:17.729
any reason.

00:07:17.729 --> 00:07:20.880
And if they want to tell the advertiser why
they're doing it, they can.

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:23.629
They want to keep their mouth shut and just
vote with their dollars.

00:07:23.629 --> 00:07:25.419
They can do that as well.

00:07:25.419 --> 00:07:30.659
Advertisers, if they start hearing, hey, you're
advertising with Tucker Carlson, we're not

00:07:30.659 --> 00:07:31.979
going to buy your stuff.

00:07:31.979 --> 00:07:36.509
The advertiser is free to listen to those
complaints or to ignore them.

00:07:36.509 --> 00:07:40.490
And the advertiser is free to say we're going
to buy more ads with Tucker or we're going

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:43.030
to cancel our ads with Tucker.

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:44.879
This is not coercion.

00:07:44.879 --> 00:07:46.270
This is not oppressive.

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:50.710
You're going to hear some people pop up concern,
trolling, saying, you know, when you email

00:07:50.710 --> 00:07:54.629
a company and say, I will not buy your stuff
if you do X or Y.

00:07:54.629 --> 00:08:00.750
That's coercion because you're holding them
economically hostage and their employees economic

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:01.750
lives.

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:03.249
Guys, come on.

00:08:03.249 --> 00:08:04.349
It's speech.

00:08:04.349 --> 00:08:05.349
It's speech.

00:08:05.349 --> 00:08:06.349
There's no violence.

00:08:06.349 --> 00:08:07.349
It's a marketplace.

00:08:07.349 --> 00:08:11.599
This is literally what the free marketeers
claim to want.

00:08:11.599 --> 00:08:17.930
So that's like 98 percent of boycott activism,
which some people call cancel culture.

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:18.939
It's just speech.

00:08:18.939 --> 00:08:23.659
It's speech you might not like, but it's speech
from people expressing how they believe others

00:08:23.659 --> 00:08:29.590
should spend dollars or spend time on social
media or go or not go to a movie based on

00:08:29.590 --> 00:08:32.279
the actors or the producers or whatever the
case may be.

00:08:32.279 --> 00:08:33.740
That's 98 percent.

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:34.740
OK.

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:39.009
Then you get to the slice of cancel culture
or boycott activism.

00:08:39.009 --> 00:08:40.600
That can be problematic.

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:48.750
So there are instances where maybe somebody
said something wrong, offensive, discriminatory

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:56.579
20 years ago and it comes out now and incomes
an angry mob and says fire them, cancel them.

00:08:56.579 --> 00:09:02.880
Where I come down on that slice is let's just
be smart instead of stupid.

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:04.079
Let's use our judgment.

00:09:04.079 --> 00:09:09.940
If someone said something stupid at some point
and it now came out and they've apologized

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:15.579
and they don't stand by it, and it's a credible
apology, and we believe based on their public

00:09:15.579 --> 00:09:20.610
facing persona, that the thing they said 20
years ago is not representative of what they

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:22.329
believe today.

00:09:22.329 --> 00:09:27.110
Then why would we go and try to get them canceled,
so to speak?

00:09:27.110 --> 00:09:29.100
There's no hard and fast rule here, right?

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:34.420
When Justin Trudeau was uncovered in blackface
in a Facebook.

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:36.800
A school yearbook.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:37.800
Rather not a Facebook.

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:40.170
A school yearbook from 30 years ago.

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:41.170
He apologized.

00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:42.300
He said it was wrong to do it.

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:43.470
That it was immature.

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:45.209
His modern platform.

00:09:45.209 --> 00:09:48.290
In Canada as a politician speaks for itself.

00:09:48.290 --> 00:09:51.779
And it has nothing to do with somebody who
believes blackface is OK.

00:09:51.779 --> 00:09:54.430
So he did a dumb thing and he took responsibility.

00:09:54.430 --> 00:09:57.279
Why would he go and try to cancel Justin Trudeau?

00:09:57.279 --> 00:10:05.079
Contrast that with year after year after year
of Tucker Carlson, kowtowing to xenophobes,

00:10:05.079 --> 00:10:07.800
throwing out white supremacist red meat.

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:09.430
Immigrants make the country dirtier.

00:10:09.430 --> 00:10:12.230
And, you know, I can't give you all the examples.

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:14.000
So there it makes sense to me.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:18.649
Oh, this is a concerted this is a distinguishable
pattern.

00:10:18.649 --> 00:10:23.060
I can see why people would want to target
his advertisers and say we won't do business

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:25.339
with you if you do business with this guy.

00:10:25.339 --> 00:10:27.810
Now, let me deal with just one last part of
this.

00:10:27.810 --> 00:10:30.930
We've we've dealt with like 98 percent.

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:32.399
And then like another one and a half.

00:10:32.399 --> 00:10:35.519
We've got like the tiniest sliver left.

00:10:35.519 --> 00:10:41.350
The anti cancel culture crowd will often insist.

00:10:41.350 --> 00:10:48.320
People's careers are being unfairly destroyed
at the drop of a of a tweet, so to speak,

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:52.589
on a whim, because they said something not
politically correct or not in line with the

00:10:52.589 --> 00:10:56.000
